# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
#
# The interface org.velarx.StorageFeed: what the engine offers a storage driver
# during a pull, served by the engine on the socket pair that BeginPull of
# org.velarx.Storage hands the driver (the descriptor 0), for that pull only; the
# driver is its client.  Generated from the Rust types
# (crates/storage-client/src/feed_proto.rs and interface.rs) by the test
# `interface::tests::the_feed_file_is_current`:
# VELARX_WRITE_INTERFACES=1 cargo test -p velarx-storage-client --lib interface
# writes it.
#
# The driver reads the index of each layer (Index), works out what it lacks
# against its own store, and asks for sets of missing files (Fetch, at most
# 4096 keys); the engine merges the ranges by cost, fetches from the sources and
# peers first, checks every chunk against the table of contents, and writes the
# bytes into the files the driver passed.  The engine holds the credentials: no
# URL, repository or digest of a blob is accepted from the driver, only keys of
# the tables of contents of the layers of this pull.
#
# Versions: GetInfo is the first call; the `proto` is the one of
# org.velarx.Storage (2).  Limits: a message is at most 1048576 bytes and
# carries at most 16 descriptors.
#
# Parallelism: one connection for each concurrent Fetch (Connect), at most
# `max_fetches` of the limits.  Back-pressure: the driver asks when it has room, and a driver
# that stops reading the replies stalls its own fetch until the idle deadline of
# the pull.  Cancellation: the engine closes the feed when the pull ends or is
# cancelled; the driver closes a Fetch connection and the engine stops that
# call's requests.  The end: after BeginPull replied `staged` the engine makes
# no more calls on the feed but keeps it open until its Commit or Abort of the
# pull has returned, so a feed that the engine closed before it decided is a
# pull nobody will decide (its engine died or gave up): the driver may end it
# and free what it holds at once, as an Abort, and need not look.
#
# The proof: the engine proves the diff id of each layer after BeginPull replies
# `staged` and before anything is published, by reading the layer back from the
# driver (ReadObjects of org.velarx.Storage): the bytes are verified chunk by
# chunk as they are fetched, and the driver may have changed them.
#
# The write: a Fetch is checked (the layer, the keys, the files, the wants and
# the budget), planned by the engine for the wants alone, fetched (every chunk
# checked against the table of contents), and written into the files the
# driver passed, each object at its place: in the file
# `Want.file` names, else in the pack file (the descriptor 0) at an offset the
# engine chooses, a multiple of 4096.  The events tell the driver as the
# objects become whole: `zeros` (the holes of an object, already holes in the
# file), `ready` (the place of each whole object, read back by the engine and
# checked to be what its key names, its fs-verity digest or the hash of its
# content: Region.fd is the position of its file among the descriptors of the
# call), `complete` (their positions among
# the wants), `failed` (the wants that were not written, and the failure: its class says
# `no_space` for the free space of the spool, `invalid` for a chunk that is not what
# the table of contents says, the class of the party that failed otherwise); the
# last reply has no event.  A
# driver that announces `objects` (org.velarx.Storage) is also told `copy`: bytes
# of an object it asked for that another object it holds has (the engine asked
# HasObjects for the objects the chunks of its records are in), which the driver
# copies itself, from `from_offset` of its object `from` to `at` of the want.
# The `copy` events of a want come before its `ready` and `complete`; the
# copied ranges are not written by the engine, and the engine does not read
# the object back at the fetch (it reads it back when it proves the layer, and
# checks it against the key it is staged under).  An object that a fetcher
# staged and the engine verified (once: the kernel's measure of its fs-verity
# digest, else one read) is not written to a place of the driver: `staged`
# hands the file itself over, a descriptor of the message at the position
# `Handed.fd` with the fs-verity digest the engine verified, and `complete`
# follows.  The driver keeps the file its own way: a link of the inode where
# its store is in the same file system, else a copy of its own; it need not
# read it.  A file that went by `staged` is no `ready`.
#
# A layer that has no table of contents in a registry (gzip, zstd without
# zstd:chunked metadata, a layer the engine made itself by a commit, a
# build step or an import) is no call of its own: the engine reads it
# whole before BeginPull, into a spool of its own, makes the table of
# contents and the tar-split from the headers and checks the blob and
# the diff id, and the driver sees it as any other layer: Index, then
# Fetch for the files it lacks, which the engine copies from the spool,
# each checked against its key (the holes of a file are told as `zeros`).
# The spool of a layer is as large as the layer: when the driver has every file
# of a layer it lacks it says so (Release) and the engine frees the spool, or
# it goes with the pull.  The engine checks the free space of the directory of
# the spool as it grows and refuses the layer naming the directory and the
# bytes it needs.
# Who may call: a peer of the same user or root (SO_PEERCRED); any
# accepted peer may make every call.

interface org.velarx.StorageFeed

# What kind of failure, for the party that reads it: what to do next.
type FailureClass (
	# This party does not have the item or cannot serve it now; nothing
	# is wrong: the engine asks the next one
	unavailable,
	# It tried and failed in a way that may pass (a timeout, a cut
	# connection, a server error): the engine asks the next one; the
	# call may be tried again
	transient,
	# What it brought, or what it was handed, is not what it claims (a
	# digest, a size, a table of contents): the operation fails and names
	# this party; nobody else is asked
	invalid,
	# The origin says the item does not exist; from a party that is not
	# the origin it is read as `unavailable`
	not_found,
	# Credentials missing or refused: the engine may `Renew` and ask again
	unauthorized,
	# The credentials were taken and do not allow it
	forbidden,
	# No space where the bytes go
	no_space,
	# It tried and failed for good (also what a value this side does not
	# know is read as)
	failed
)

# Why something failed.
type Failure (class: FailureClass, reason: string)

# A layer of the pull that the driver has to bring in.
type FeedLayer (
	# The key of the layer, as `BeginPull` names it
	key: string,
	diff_id: string,
	# The bytes of its `toc.zst` (the first file of `Index`)
	toc_length: int,
	# The bytes of its compressed tar-split (the second file of `Index`); 0
	# for a layer whose table of contents has `"version": 2`, the canonical
	# tar of its entries
	tarsplit_length: int,
	# The entries of the table of contents
	entries: int,
	# The bytes of the files of the layer
	size: int
)

# A file the driver lacks: its key in the table of contents and its size.
type Want (
	key: string,
	size: int,
	# The position among the files of the call of one that holds this object
	# alone, from offset 0.  Without it the object goes into the pack file
	# (the file 0)
	file: ?int
)

# Bytes of a file the message carries: `length` bytes from `offset`.
type Region (
	# The position of the file among the descriptors of the message it is
	# about: the call, for the events of a `Fetch`
	fd: int,
	offset: int,
	length: int
)

# Bytes `[at, at + region.length)` of the object `want` of the call, in a region
# of a file the message carries.
type Piece (
	# The position of the object among the wants of the call
	want: int,
	at: int,
	region: Region
)

# Bytes of the object `want` that another object of the driver holds: the
# driver copies them itself.
type Copy (
	want: int,
	at: int,
	# The key of the object the bytes are in
	from: string,
	from_offset: int,
	length: int
)

# A file the engine hands over for the object `want`: the object, complete
# and verified by the engine, is the file the message carries, which the
# driver keeps its own way.
type Handed (
	# The position of the object among the wants of the call
	want: int,
	# The position of the file among the descriptors of this message
	fd: int,
	# The fs-verity digest of the object, which the engine verified
	verity: string
)

# A hole of the object `want`: `length` bytes of zeros from `at`.
type Zeros (want: int, at: int, length: int)

# What an event of a `Fetch` tells.
type EventKind (
	# `pieces`: bytes are in the regions
	ready,
	# `handed`: the objects are whole and verified, in the files the
	# message carries
	staged,
	# `copies`: the driver copies them from its own objects
	copy,
	# `zeros`: holes
	zeros,
	# `wants`: objects that are whole, their keys verified
	complete,
	# `wants` could not be fetched, `failure` says why
	failed,
	# An event this side does not know: it is ignored
	unknown
)

# What an event of a `Fetch` tells.
type FetchEvent (
	kind: EventKind,
	pieces: []Piece,
	copies: []Copy,
	handed: []Handed,
	zeros: []Zeros,
	# With `complete` and `failed`: the wants, by position in the call
	wants: []int,
	# With `failed`: why
	failure: ?Failure
)

# The shared `GetInfo` (`limits` has `max_wants`, `max_fetches` and
# `max_fetch_bytes`), the pull and its layers.
method GetInfo() -> (
	name: string,
	version: string,
	# The version of the protocol
	proto: int,
	capabilities: []string,
	limits: [string]int,
	# The pull, as `BeginPull` names it
	pull: string,
	# The layers the driver has to bring in: those of the pull that are not
	# stored, each once
	layers: []FeedLayer
)

# Another connection to this feed, for a parallel `Fetch`: the reply
# carries its descriptor, the end of a socket pair served as this
# one.  `TooMuch` at `max_fetches`.
method Connect() -> ()

# The two files of the layer as the engine keeps them, as sealed
# memory files of the lengths `GetInfo` says: `toc.zst` (descriptor
# 0), the table of the layer as the registry sent it, or the zstd of
# the JSON of its table of contents, whose entries are those of the
# verified table as a layer keeps them (the names normalised, the
# permission bits only, no attribute of the overlay file system);
# and the zstd compressed tar-split (descriptor 1) of a `chunked`
# layer.  The driver keeps both as they are: the engine reads them
# back with `LayerFiles`.
method Index(layer: string) -> ()

# The driver has every file of `layer` that it lacks: the engine
# frees what it keeps to serve the layer (the spool of a layer it
# read whole), and a `Fetch` of the layer after it is `Failed`.
# Idempotent; nothing for a layer that keeps nothing.
method Release(layer: string) -> ()

# Fetch `wants` (at most `max_wants` of the limits, keys of the table of contents
# of `layer`, each once), all planned and merged together by the
# engine.  Called with `more`.  The call carries the files the
# engine writes the bytes into: the descriptor 0 is the pack file,
# an empty regular file open for writing (or the driver's memory
# file), then up to 15 files of one object each (`Want.file`).  A
# file must be a regular, empty file on a file system whose writes
# cannot be held up by another process (not FUSE, not a network file
# system): the engine writes into it in line, and a write that never
# returns would hold up every pull of the engine.  The engine keeps
# no descriptor after the call.  The events: `ready` (pieces),
# `copy` and `zeros` (the driver's own bytes and holes), `complete`
# (objects whole, their keys verified) and `failed` (with the
# failure); the last reply has no event and `continues: false`, and
# an event this side does not know is ignored.  The feed is scoped to the pull: a key that is not
# in the table of contents of the layer is `UnknownObject`, and the
# total asked for is at most twice the planned bytes of the layer
# (`TooMuch`).  Closing the connection cancels the call.
method Fetch(layer: string, wants: []Want) -> (event: ?FetchEvent)

# A layer that is not one of the pull
error UnknownLayer (layer: string)

# A key that is not in the table of contents of the layer, or whose
# size is not the one it states
error UnknownObject (key: string)

# A file of the call that is not an empty regular file open for
# writing, on a file system whose writes may block (FUSE, a network file
# system), or that the wants name wrongly
error BadFile (reason: string)

# The budget of the pull (twice the planned bytes of the layer), the
# wants of a call, the calls at the same time
error TooMuch (reason: string)

# The engine tried and failed, or cannot place the bytes
error Failed (failure: Failure)
